, 0.0282135009765625, 0.02099609375, -5.1975250244140625e-05, -0.037445068359375, -0.032745361328125, 0.01273345947265625, -0.018463134765625, -0.00849151611328125, 0.040679931640625, 0.0275726318359375, 0.042633056640625, 0.0204010009765625, 0.02288818359375, 0.028533935546875, 0.01448822021484375, -0.037628173828125, 0.00022709369659423828, 0.00705718994140625, 0.0206298828125, 0.00841522216796875, -0.00501251220703125, 0.007366180419921875, -0.020233154296875, -0.0301361083984375, 0.0196533203125, -0.0102691650390625, -0.00019347667694091797, 0.01148223876953125, -0.01454925537109375, -0.0168304443359375, 0.0052337646484375, -0.0222625732421875, -0.0202484130859375, 0.02191162109375, 0.0007905960083007812, 0.0250091552734375, 0.00847625732421875, -0.02392578125, -0.0173492431640625, 0.0035839080810546875, 0.029541015625, -0.015838623046875, 0.0034236907958984375, 0.0159149169921875, -0.00554656982421875, 0.01186370849609375, -0.01538848876953125, -0.014068603515625, 0.037322998046875, -0.01546478271484375, 0.00988006591796875, 0.01538848876953125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "# Introduction\n\nGeometric Algebra (Geometric Algebra) is a mathematical framework that extends traditional vector algebra by introducing new elements called multivectors, which include scalars, vectors, bivectors (representing oriented planes), trivectors, and higher-grade elements. Geometric Algebra provides a unified language for representing geometric transformations and relationships, allowing for more natural and compact descriptions of complex systems in mathematics, physics, and engineering. Its algebraic structure encompasses both commutative and non-commutative operations, making it particularly powerful for modeling rotations, reflections, and other geometric operations [5].\n\n**Objective:** In this work, we propose a systematic extension of the Laplace transform to multivector-valued functions in Geometric Algebra. Existing Laplace transform extensions are typically limited to specific algebraic structures (such as quaternions or commutative subalgebras) or to functions of a single grade, and they often cannot handle the non-commutative multiplication and mixed-grade elements that arise in full Geometric Algebra. Our approach enables the direct transformation and analysis of systems modeled with full multivector representations, overcoming these limitations and allowing for the analysis and control of complex systems in engineering and physics where Geometric Algebra provides a natural modeling language.\n\n**Notation:** Throughout this document, we use the notation (p, q) to denote the signature of a Geometric Algebra, where p is the number of positive-square basis vectors and q is the number of negative-square basis vectors. The sum p + q gives the dimension of the underlying vector space. For example, in three-dimensional space, p + q = 3. The proposed method is applicable for p + q \u2264 5, as explained below.\n\n# Glossary of Key Terms\n\n- **Multivector-valued function:** A function whose values are multivectors, i.e., sums of elements of different grades (scalars, vectors, bivectors, etc.) in a geometric algebra.\n- **Non-commutative multiplication:** In Geometric Algebra, the product of two multivectors does not necessarily commute; that is, for multivectors \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), \\(AB\\) may not equal \\(BA\\).\n- **Grade:** The degree of a basis element in Geometric Algebra, e.g., scalars (grade 0), vectors (grade 1), bivectors (grade 2), etc.\n\n# Motivation and Limitations of Existing Methods\n\nThe Laplace transform is a fundamental tool in engineering and physics for converting time-domain differential equations into algebraic equations in the frequency domain. However, traditional Laplace transforms are defined only for real- or complex-valued functions, making them unsuitable for direct application to multivector-valued functions or the algebraic structures present in Geometric Algebra. Previous attempts to extend the Laplace transform to hyper-complex algebras, such as quaternions and Clifford algebras [1,3], often focus on specific algebraic structures or commutative subalgebras, and may not generalize to the full range of multivector elements in Geometric Algebra. For example, Adler [1] restricts attention to quaternionic functions, which correspond to a specific 4D subalgebra, while Hitzer [3] often considers commutative subalgebras or specific grades in Clifford algebras. These approaches can struggle with the non-commutative properties inherent in Geometric Algebra and may restrict the transform to certain grades, limiting their applicability in practical engineering and physics problems where full multivector representations are required.\n\n**Illustrative Example:**  \nConsider the analysis of a three-phase electrical circuit where voltages and currents are naturally represented as vectors and bivectors in Geometric Algebra. Suppose the system equations involve the outer product \\( V(t) \\wedge I(t) \\), where \\( V(t) \\) and \\( I(t) \\) are vector-valued functions representing voltages and currents, respectively. In traditional approaches, one would need to decompose \\( V(t) \\) and \\( I(t) \\) into their scalar components, apply the Laplace transform to each component separately, and then attempt to reconstruct the geometric product in the Laplace domain. This process is cumbersome and risks losing the geometric meaning and relationships between different grades. For example, if \\( V(t) = v_1(t)\\mathbf{e}_1 + v_2(t)\\mathbf{e}_2 + v_3(t)\\mathbf{e}_3 \\) and \\( I(t) = i_1(t)\\mathbf{e}_1 + i_2(t)\\mathbf{e}_2 + i_3(t)\\mathbf{e}_3 \\), the outer product \\( V(t) \\wedge I(t) \\) produces a bivector-valued function involving terms like \\( (v_1(t)i_2(t) - v_2(t)i_1(t))\\mathbf{e}_1\\mathbf{e}_2 \\). Existing Laplace transform extensions cannot directly handle such mixed-grade, non-commutative products. In contrast, the proposed method allows direct transformation of the entire multivector equation, preserving both the grade structure and the non-commutative multiplication rules of Geometric Algebra.\n\nSimilarly, in rigid body dynamics, angular velocities and torques are best modeled as bivectors, but current Laplace transform methods cannot accommodate the full algebraic structure, limiting their utility in control and analysis tasks [2,4].\n\n# Comparison with Existing Methods\n\n| Feature                                 | Traditional Laplace | Quaternionic/Clifford Extensions [1,3] | Proposed Geometric Algebra Method |\n|------------------------------------------|--------------------|----------------------------------------|----------------------------------|\n| Handles mixed-grade multivectors         | No                 | Limited (single grade or subalgebra)   | Yes                             |\n| Preserves non-commutative multiplication | No                 | Partially                              | Yes                             |\n| Applicable to full Geometric Algebra     | No                 | No                                     | Yes (p+q \u2264 5)                   |\n| Direct transformation of products        | No                 | No                                     | Yes                             |\n| Computational tractability               | Yes                | Yes (low dimension)                    | Yes (p+q \u2264 5)                   |\n\n# Background\n\nRecent publications have explored the application of Geometric Algebra to the modeling, analysis, and control of dynamical systems, especially in the context of electrical circuits and mechanical systems [2,4,6,7]. For example, Doran and Lasenby [6] provide a comprehensive introduction to Geometric Algebra in physics and engineering, while Hestenes and Sobczyk [7] discuss its applications in mechanics and electromagnetism. A key aspect of system analysis is the transformation of ordinary differential equations (ODEs) from the real domain to the Laplace domain. The Laplace transform simplifies the analysis of linear systems and enables the use of algebraic techniques for system design and stability analysis. However, as noted above, existing Laplace transform extensions do not fully address the needs of multivector-valued functions in Geometric Algebra, particularly when non-commutative multiplication and higher-grade elements are involved [1,3].\n\n# Proposed Method\n\nWe introduce a definition of the Laplace transform specifically tailored for Geometric Algebra, building upon earlier efforts in the literature [1,3]. Our approach is applicable to geometric algebras with signature (p, q) where the sum p + q \u2264 5. This limitation is chosen because the number of basis elements in Geometric Algebra grows exponentially as 2^(p+q), and the complexity of multivector multiplication increases rapidly with dimension. For example, in a 5-dimensional Geometric Algebra (p + q = 5), there are 32 basis elements, while in a 6-dimensional Geometric Algebra, there are 64. This exponential growth makes explicit construction and manipulation of the Laplace transform computationally intensive for p + q > 5, which currently limits practical implementation with available computational tools. In most engineering and physics applications, such as three-phase circuits (modeled in 3D Geometric Algebra, p+q=3) or rigid body dynamics (modeled in 3D or 4D Geometric Algebra, p+q=3 or 4), the relevant systems can be modeled within this dimensionality, ensuring the method's utility for a broad range of practical problems.\n\n## Method Overview\n\nOur method systematically extends the Laplace transform to multivector-valued functions by:\n\n- **Component-wise Transformation:** The Laplace transform is defined for each grade of the multivector separately, ensuring that scalars, vectors, bivectors, and higher-grade elements are all transformed appropriately. For a general multivector-valued function \\( f(t) = \\sum_{k=0}^n f_k(t) \\), where each \\( f_k(t) \\) is the grade-k component, the Laplace transform is \\( \\mathcal{L}[f(t)] = \\sum_{k=0}^n \\mathcal{L}[f_k(t)] \\).\n- **Preservation of Algebraic Structure:** The transformation preserves the algebraic structure of Geometric Algebra, including the non-commutative nature of multivector multiplication. This is achieved by ensuring that the basis elements and their multiplication rules are maintained in the Laplace domain.\n- **Order of Multiplication:** The order of multiplication is strictly preserved during the transformation process. For products of multivectors, the Laplace transform is applied to the product as a whole, not to the individual factors, so that \\( \\mathcal{L}[A(t)B(t)] = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st}A(t)B(t)dt \\), with multiplication inside the integral following Geometric Algebra rules.\n- **Handling Non-commutativity:** In general, \\( \\mathcal{L}[A(t)B(t)] \\neq \\mathcal{L}[A(t"}}